<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hernán Cortés</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1485">1485</a>–<a href="/years/1547">1547</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Cortés</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hernán Cortés was a Spanish conquistador who led the expedition that brought down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> between 1519 and 1521. Allied with indigenous peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a>, he captured the Aztec capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. His conquest was a founding event of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes.jpg" alt="Hernán Cortés" width="320" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Hern%C3%A1n_Cort%C3%A9s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hernán Cortés was born around 1485 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medellin">Medellín</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>, to Martín Cortés, an hidalgo of distinguished ancestry but slender means, and Catalina Pizarro; through his mother he was a distant relation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>. At fourteen he was sent to study <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> with an uncle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a>. He sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> in 1504, registered as a citizen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, received an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> and became a notary at Azua de Compostela. In 1511 he accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</a> in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, where he served as clerk to the treasurer and became a magistrate of the second Spanish town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1518 Velázquez put Cortés in command of an expedition to the mainland, then revoked his charter at the last minute. Cortés ignored the order and sailed in February 1519 with about 11 ships, 500 men, 13 horses and a few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. On the Yucatán coast he found Geronimo de Aguilar, a Spanish priest who had learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chontal-maya">Chontal Maya</a>, and he also used Doña Marina as his interpreter. He allied with some native peoples against others. In mid-August 1519 he marched on Tenochtitlán with 600 soldiers, 15 horsemen, 15 cannons and hundreds of indigenous carriers and warriors, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a> had repeatedly refused to meet him.</p><p>Cortés's force reached Tlaxcala after the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otumba">Otumba</a>, having lost 870 men. With their allies and reinforcements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, they prevailed. Cortés cut off supplies, subdued the cities allied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> and built brigantines on the lake. He destroyed the city block by block, and the siege ended in Spanish victory and the destruction of Tenochtitlán. He wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> asking to be rewarded rather than punished for mutiny, and received the title of Marquis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a>, while the viceroyalty went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a> in 1535. From 1524 to 1526 he led an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, during which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a> was executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cortés returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1541, hoping to answer lawsuits brought by his opponents, and joined the emperor's expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, in which he nearly drowned in a storm. Heavily in debt after financing his own expeditions, he made a claim on the royal treasury in February 1544 and was ignored for three years. He set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1547 but was taken ill at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and died at Castilleja de la Cuesta on 2 December 1547, aged 62. His letters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, the cartas de relación, are his only surviving writings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> was built on the ruins of Tenochtitlán, and the Paso de Cortés, the pass between the volcanoes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iztaccihuatl">Iztaccíhuatl</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popocatepetl">Popocatépetl</a> through which he led his soldiers, bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hernán Cortés” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The system's strategic weakness lay in its relationship with its tributaries: the coercive extraction that financed the army also created a large pool of resentful subject peoples who needed only a credible alternative leadership to defect, which <b>Hernán Cortés</b> provided in 1519.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Habsburgs Castilian infantry formed the core of the tercios that fought in Italy and Flanders, while Castilian conquistadors such as <b>Hernán Cortés</b> and Francisco Pizarro overthrew the Aztec and Inca empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same moment <b>Hernán Cortés</b> began the conquest of the Aztec Empire (1519–1521), and Francisco Pizarro later toppled the Inca Empire (1532–1533).</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hernán Cortés</b> landed on the Gulf coast in 1519, founded Veracruz, and with the decisive help of indigenous allies, above all the Tlaxcalans, besieged Tenochtitlan, which fell on 13 August 1521 after a smallpox epidemic had already devastated its defenders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of Tenochtitlan, <b>Hernán Cortés</b> sent Cristóbal de Olid into Michoacán, and in the summer of 1522 Olid entered Tzintzuntzan without a battle, while the Spaniards gathered gold and silver from its temples and royal tombs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The conquest of Mexico by <b>Hernán Cortés</b> (1519–1521) and of Peru by Francisco Pizarro from 1532 turned Spain from a Caribbean colonial power into the master of the two wealthiest indigenous states of the Americas, and brought the gold and silver of the Aztec and Inca treasuries into…</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Medellín, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Castilleja de la Cuesta, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.96,-5.96&amp;year=1525" aria-label="Medellín on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4402 766 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hernán Cortés"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4852 1036h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4852 1036h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.96,-5.96&amp;year=1525">What was here in 1525? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I and my fellowes haue a certayne disease of the harte, and golde helpeth vs.&#8221;</p><footer>To Montezuma's envoy Teudilli, 1519 (Gómara's history) &#183; tr. Thomas Nicholas, 1578 &#183; reported by Francisco López de Gómara, his chaplain and biographer &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46524/pg46524-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…if ye forsake me not, as I will not forsake you, I will with Gods help make you in shorte time the richest men that euer passed this way.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to his men before sailing from Cuba, 1519 (Gómara's history) &#183; tr. Thomas Nicholas, 1578 &#183; reported by Francisco López de Gómara, his chaplain and biographer &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46524/pg46524-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Maisters and louyng friendes, I did choose you for my fellowes, and ye chose me for your captaine, and all was for the seruice of God…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to his wavering men, 1519 (Gómara's history) &#183; tr. Thomas Nicholas, 1578 &#183; reported by Francisco López de Gómara, his chaplain and biographer &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46524/pg46524-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for my commyng hither is to take away all euill customes, and to helpe the oppressed, to fauour the prysoner, and comforte the afflicted, and chiefly to abolishe tyrannie.&#8221;</p><footer>To the envoys of the Totonacs, 1519 (Gómara's history) &#183; tr. Thomas Nicholas, 1578 &#183; reported by Francisco López de Gómara, his chaplain and biographer &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46524/pg46524-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…you knowe, that when I came into thys Countrey, I commaunded all my Shippes to be sonke, so that nowe I haue néede of time conueniente to builde vessels to carrie vs into oure Countrey…&#8221;</p><footer>To Moctezuma, 1520 (Gómara's history) &#183; tr. Thomas Nicholas, 1578 &#183; reported by Francisco López de Gómara, his chaplain and biographer &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46524/pg46524-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
